# PII denylist — personal terms that must never land in this repo.
#
# THIS TEMPLATE SHIPS EMPTY ON PURPOSE. It carries no terms, and it never
# should: a denylist committed to a repo IS the leak it exists to prevent.
# The installer copies this file to `pii-denylist.local` at your repo root,
# which is gitignored. You fill that copy in; it stays on your machine.
#
# One term per line; matching is case-insensitive and fixed-string. No wildcards
# — list each variant (diacritic and ASCII spellings) as its own term.
#
# Matching is WHOLE-WORD (a term inside a longer word doesn't match) everywhere
# a layer reads CONTENT: staged diffs, pushed history, and both CI content scans.
#
# ONE EXCEPTION — CI also scans tracked file PATHS, and that scan is a SUBSTRING
# match on purpose: a filename concatenates where prose does not, so a
# whole-word rule would miss `assets/<term>scan.png`. Practical consequence: the
# term `Ana` fails CI on a file named `banana.png`. That job logs counts only,
# never the path, so pick terms with this in mind — short terms that are common
# substrings will bite here and nowhere else.
#
# Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are ignored.
#
# The hooks self-protect: force-staging your filled-in copy would match its
# own terms and block the commit.
#
# After editing, the LOCAL hooks use the new terms immediately — nothing to
# run. The CI secret PII_DENYLIST does NOT update by itself: pushing only
# WARNS when this file has drifted from the last value written to it. To
# update CI, run
#   .githooks/overwrite-ci-denylist
# deliberately (needs an authenticated gh and a terminal; typed confirmation).
# Operator-run on purpose: an Actions secret is write-only, so an overwrite
# destroys a value that nobody — no API, no admin — can read back first.
#
# Put the people this repo touches first, then family names / any personal
# context that must never land in the tree.
